WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.700
PAUL JAY: Welcome back to The Real News Network.
This is Reality Asserts Itself, and I'm Paul

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:13.250
Jay.

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:17.029
We're continuing our series of interviews
with John Kiriakou, who joins us again in

00:00:17.029 --> 00:00:17.340
the studio.

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:18.050
Thanks for joining us.

00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:18.920
JOHN C. KIRIAKOU: Thanks for having me.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:24.419
JAY: So, one more time, John's a former CIA
analyst and then became a caseworker. And

00:00:24.419 --> 00:00:29.730
he's author of the book The Reluctant Spy:
My Secret Life in the CIA's War on Terror.

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:35.320
He spent about 23 months in jail and is now
under house arrest for another 40-odd days

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:40.019
for being the only person convicted of having
anything to do with the torture program in

00:00:40.019 --> 00:00:44.690
the United States. And, no, he wasn't convicted
for torturing anybody; he was convicted for

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:45.280
whistleblowing.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:47.679
Thanks for joining us.

00:00:47.679 --> 00:00:49.480
KIRIAKOU: My pleasure.

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:52.149
JAY: How did you get recruited by the CIA?

00:00:52.149 --> 00:00:59.149
KIRIAKOU: In grad school I took a class called
"Leadership", very simply. It was taught by

00:00:59.839 --> 00:01:06.839
an eminent psychiatrist by the name of Dr.
Jerrold Post. Dr. Post is one of the country's

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:14.470
leading thinkers in the area of terrorist
psychology. So, in this class, one of our

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:24.000
assignments was to shadow our bosses and write
a psychological profile of that boss.

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:29.140
At the time, I was working at United Food
and Commercial Workers International Union

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:36.140
in Washington--long history of labor involvement
in my family. And I wrote a paper, and I said

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:44.340
that I believed my boss was a sociopath and
that he was potentially dangerous. And I offered

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:48.600
evidence to show why I thought he was potentially
dangerous.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:54.090
So I got an A on this paper. And in the margin,
Dr. Post wrote, I've never done this before,

00:01:54.090 --> 00:02:00.480
but I urge you to resign from this job. I
too believe that your boss is a sociopath.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:03.160
So I quit and got another job.

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:09.450
In the meantime, he asked me to stay after
class one day, and he said, "Do you have any

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:16.130
job lined up after graduation?" And I said,
"Oh, Dr. Post, not only do I not have a job;

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:19.670
I don't even have any prospects for a job,
and I'm getting married a couple of weeks

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:26.130
after graduation." So he said, "Have you ever
given any thought to the CIA?" And I said,

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:33.130
"No, not really." "Do you think you'd be interested?"
I said, "Sure. I have nothing else going on."

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:41.860
And so he took me back to his office, went
through his Rolodex (in the days when everybody

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:48.190
had Rolodex). And as he was going through
the Rolodex, I remember seeing one card that

00:02:48.190 --> 00:02:53.870
said, "Ollie North - Home", and it had a number.
And I thought, who is this guy that he has

00:02:53.870 --> 00:03:00.180
Oliver North's home phone number in his Rolodex?
And this is pretty much in the midst of Iran-Contra.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:07.180
We're just after the end of Iran-Contra. So
he calls a number and says, "Bob, are you

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:14.780
free? I have a good one," and hung up the
phone and said, "You need to go to Rosslyn,

00:03:15.569 --> 00:03:21.930
the Rosslyn Metro station in Arlington, Virginia,
go to this address, and ask for Bob."

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:28.930
So I got on the subway, took it one stop to
Rosslyn. And I went to this completely nondescript

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:36.770
building that no longer exists and went up
to the sixth floor, and I buzzed a buzzer,

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:42.750
and a uniformed cop of some sort came out
and said, "Are you here for Bob?" I said,

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:48.680
"I am." So he lets me into a little atrium.
And there's another door that has a big spinlock

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:55.680
on it and a cipher, like a bank vault. So
he buzzes that door, and it opens, and a woman

00:03:56.390 --> 00:04:00.280
pops her head out--just her head--and she
says, "Are you here for Bob?" I said, "I'm

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:03.400
here for Bob." So she lets me in.

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:10.400
And a minute later, this huge, six-foot-five,
350-pound guy comes out, and with his hand

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:18.650
out says, "Hi. I'm Bob. Are you John?" I said,
"I am." "Come on in here, John." And I talked

00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:24.800
to him for a few minutes. And he said, "What
I want you to do is Saturday morning at 8

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:29.659
o'clock be at the George Washington University
medical center auditorium. You're going to

00:04:29.659 --> 00:04:32.870
take some tests." I said okay.

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:39.870
So that Saturday morning, I went into town
and went to the medical school auditorium.

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:47.830
There were 200 people there at least. And
the proctors handed out these stacks of papers.

00:04:48.949 --> 00:04:55.330
So one was a big map of the world, but it
was just blank. All the countries were divided,

00:04:55.330 --> 00:04:59.710
but it was blank, and you had write in the
name of every country. And I say in my book,

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:06.089
I was always a map nut. So I just scribbled
in the names of all the countries. And the

00:05:06.089 --> 00:05:12.419
next one was a current events test, multiple-choice.
Crazy easy. Like, you didn't even have to

00:05:12.419 --> 00:05:18.509
subscribe to a newspaper at the time. If you
had just walked by the box holding The Washington

00:05:18.509 --> 00:05:23.199
Post and looked at the top half of the front
page, you could have done well on this test.

00:05:23.199 --> 00:05:25.759
It was very easy. I finished that quickly.

00:05:25.759 --> 00:05:31.599
And then the first test was very long, several
thousand questions. The questions were all

00:05:31.599 --> 00:05:34.620
yes or no.

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:39.900
And I also mention this in the book. As an
example, one of the questions was: I like

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:44.729
boxing. And the truth is, I didn't really
care one way or the other about boxing. Mike

00:05:44.729 --> 00:05:50.349
Tyson was prominent at the time, and I liked
Mike Tyson. So I thought, well, I don't really

00:05:50.349 --> 00:05:56.779
care about boxing, but if Mike Tyson's fighting,
I'll usually stop and watch it. So I put yes.

00:05:56.779 --> 00:06:03.779
But then, like, 234 questions later, it says:
I like boxing. And I thought, geez, what did

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:11.680
I say the first time? I can't remember. I
think I said yes. I think. And I put yes.

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:16.610
And then another 400 questions later, it says:
I like boxing. And I was like, geez, oh man.

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:20.029
So you see what they're doing. They're looking
for patterns.

00:06:20.029 --> 00:06:26.110
So I did it. I finished it. I finished it
first of anybody. And I sat there for a while

00:06:26.110 --> 00:06:31.199
and looked around, and nobody else was getting
up, so I thought, ah, I'm done. So I got up,

00:06:31.199 --> 00:06:36.180
I handed it to the proctor, and I walked out.
My wife picks me up and she says, how'd you

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:41.509
do? I said, I have no idea, I have no idea
how I did. I said, the questions were so weird,

00:06:41.509 --> 00:06:46.520
I'm not sure what they were looking for. A
week or so later, I get a call from Bob. He

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:53.430
says, you blew the doors off that test. I
want you to go to another equally nondescript

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:56.309
building in Vienna, Virginia, and take some
more tests.

00:06:56.309 --> 00:06:58.580
PAUL JAY, SENIOR EDITOR, TRNN: Were any of
the questions on the test political? What

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:00.300
do you think of this, what do you think of
that?

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:01.289
KIRIAKOU: No, nothing like that.

00:07:01.289 --> 00:07:03.089
JAY: It's all attitudinal.

00:07:03.089 --> 00:07:04.270
KIRIAKOU: All attitudinal.

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:10.119
The current events test was so simple it was--I
remember one of the questions was the Prime

00:07:10.119 --> 00:07:17.119
Minister of Greece is (a) Andreas Papandreou,
(b) Mao Zedong, (c) Leonid Brezhnev, or (d)

00:07:18.339 --> 00:07:22.740
U Thant. So, come on. You know, I just blew
right through it.

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:26.199
JAY: When you're thinking about joining the
CIA, first of all, it doesn't sound like you

00:07:26.199 --> 00:07:27.379
thought too much about it.

00:07:27.379 --> 00:07:28.479
KIRIAKOU: I didn't think at all about it.

00:07:28.479 --> 00:07:33.529
JAY: But did it not occur to you to think
about the Church committee, for example, that--you

00:07:33.529 --> 00:07:35.240
must have been aware (you're a savvy guy)
that--

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:35.939
KIRIAKOU: Oh, yeah. I had--.

00:07:35.939 --> 00:07:37.539
JAY: --the Church committee--. But let me
just--

00:07:37.539 --> 00:07:37.990
KIRIAKOU: Sorry.

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:41.379
JAY: --well, just for people that don't know,
I mean, the Church committee that investigated

00:07:41.379 --> 00:07:46.529
terrible misdeeds--assassination attempts--so,
I mean, terrible, terrible things that the

00:07:46.529 --> 00:07:52.939
Church committee revealed. And afterwards,
there are many investigative pieces in newspapers

00:07:52.939 --> 00:07:56.039
and magazines, and the CIA had done bad stuff.

00:07:56.039 --> 00:07:57.360
KIRIAKOU: Bad stuff.

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:00.749
JAY: That didn't ring for you, do I want to
be a part of this?

00:08:00.749 --> 00:08:05.569
KIRIAKOU: I knew a guy in college whose father
worked at the CIA, and I asked him if he would

00:08:05.569 --> 00:08:09.129
have coffee with me one day. And I asked him
questions.

00:08:09.129 --> 00:08:15.219
And then there was a woman I knew in grad
school whose boyfriend worked at the CIA.

00:08:15.219 --> 00:08:19.899
And I did the same thing with him. We had
dinner one night. And they told me that, yes,

00:08:19.899 --> 00:08:24.969
the Church committee had revealed terrible
crimes, but by God, the CIA had cleaned up

00:08:24.969 --> 00:08:29.460
its act, and it's a great place to work, and
you're going to love it, because everything

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:34.620
is good and everything's clean now and everything's
by the book. And I said, great, that sounds

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:36.430
like a place for me.

00:08:36.430 --> 00:08:38.800
JAY: And who can say no to Bob?

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:40.979
KIRIAKOU: That's right. Who could say no to
Bob?

00:08:40.979 --> 00:08:46.910
JAY: Alright. So you go to another nondescript
building. And another Bob?

00:08:46.910 --> 00:08:53.910
KIRIAKOU: No. I'm met there by three individuals:
a psychologist, a psychiatrist, and an anthropologist.

00:08:57.509 --> 00:09:03.519
And they interview me. And they're asking
me questions like, describe your relationship

00:09:03.519 --> 00:09:08.620
with your mother. And I said, well, I have
a terrific mother. You know, she's always

00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:15.480
been supportive. We've always gotten along.
And they look at each other and nod. Describe

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:20.339
your relationship with your father. Was your
father the disciplinarian in the family?

00:09:20.339 --> 00:09:25.250
And then, finally, they ask me a question
that still sticks with me: have you ever betrayed

00:09:25.250 --> 00:09:31.589
a friendship? And I said, oh my God, I hope
not. I don't think so. I said, let me think

00:09:31.589 --> 00:09:38.170
about it. They said, no, no, that's the answer
we were looking for. I said, okay. When that

00:09:38.170 --> 00:09:45.170
was done, they took hair, blood, urine, sent
me on my way. And then, a week later, Bob

00:09:46.529 --> 00:09:53.529
calls and says, you aced those tests, so we're
going to send you for interviews. So he set

00:09:54.540 --> 00:10:01.540
up a day's worth of interviews at CIA headquarters.
And a couple of weeks after that, I got an

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:03.269
offer in the mail.

00:10:03.269 --> 00:10:09.810
JAY: Now, you wrote in your book, I believe,
that they like to look for people--did you

00:10:09.810 --> 00:10:12.600
call trending towards sociopath?

00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:18.550
KIRIAKOU: Well, a CIA psychiatrist told me
one time that the CIA looks to hire people

00:10:18.550 --> 00:10:24.879
with sociopathic tendencies--not sociopaths,
because sociopaths have no consciences. But

00:10:24.879 --> 00:10:30.500
people with sociopathic tendencies are comfortable
operating in moral and legal gray areas.

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:32.389
JAY: So do you think they found that in you?

00:10:32.389 --> 00:10:38.620
KIRIAKOU: I think they probably did, because
at that time, when they asked me the question--they

00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:45.620
offered a scenario. They said, you know that
Mr. X has something in his house that you

00:10:47.459 --> 00:10:54.459
need, whether it's a file or whatever. You
need it. And you work on him to recruit him

00:10:54.560 --> 00:11:01.560
so that eventually he turns that file over
to you. But he's not recruitable. And in the

00:11:01.569 --> 00:11:06.519
end, when you ask him for the file, he tells
you, no. What do you do? I said, I break into

00:11:06.519 --> 00:11:11.180
the house and take the file. Seemed like a
perfectly logical answer to me.

00:11:11.180 --> 00:11:13.490
JAY: 'Cause they were--but you understood
they were asking you--

00:11:13.490 --> 00:11:14.000
KIRIAKOU: Oh, of course.

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:15.269
JAY: --to think like a CIA person.

00:11:15.269 --> 00:11:15.779
KIRIAKOU: Absolutely.

00:11:15.779 --> 00:11:19.509
JAY: I mean, did you believe that? Or you
were feeding them something you thought they

00:11:19.509 --> 00:11:20.440
wanted to hear?

00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:26.319
KIRIAKOU: No, I believed that. If Mr. X was
a Russian scientist, let's say, I would absolutely

00:11:26.319 --> 00:11:31.410
break into his house and take it. Right? We're
the good guys. They're the bad guys.

00:11:31.410 --> 00:11:32.180
JAY: Right.

00:11:32.180 --> 00:11:34.079
KIRIAKOU: So, sure, I believed it.

00:11:34.079 --> 00:11:39.120
JAY: So you're hired. You go through orientation.
And you write in the book that one of the

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:42.819
people that's giving you the orientation--I'm
quoting--made the biggest impression. The

00:11:42.819 --> 00:11:47.360
director of the Office of Security made the
biggest impression on you, saying something

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:52.779
that stayed with you all these years, that,
he said, the greatest threat facing America

00:11:52.779 --> 00:11:57.399
today is the threat of Soviet communism. And
you didn't think so.

00:11:57.399 --> 00:12:03.589
KIRIAKOU: I thought that was absurd. This
is January 1990, right? The Berlin wall's

00:12:03.589 --> 00:12:10.129
down, the Soviet Union is breaking up, and
Eastern Europe is turning democratic, and

00:12:10.129 --> 00:12:15.649
this old man is standing there telling us
that the greatest threat we face is Soviet

00:12:15.649 --> 00:12:21.959
communism? I thought it was ridiculous. And
I wondered if it was just for show, if it

00:12:21.959 --> 00:12:26.589
was to make us feel like, wow, we're really
part of something now. But as the years passed,

00:12:26.589 --> 00:12:30.779
I think I've come to the conclusion that he
believed it, that he was one of those old

00:12:30.779 --> 00:12:33.060
timers who just couldn't change with the times.

00:12:33.060 --> 00:12:38.519
JAY: Just the culture was too embedded. What
did you think was the greatest threat? And

00:12:38.519 --> 00:12:41.399
were you joining because you thought there
was a threat? Or you were joining 'cause it's

00:12:41.399 --> 00:12:42.250
something interesting to do?

00:12:42.250 --> 00:12:46.089
KIRIAKOU: No, I thought it would be interesting.
And, frankly, I was looking forward to the

00:12:46.089 --> 00:12:50.029
travel. That's really what was most exciting
for me.

00:12:50.029 --> 00:12:56.439
You know, I really didn't think there was
any large transnational threat at the time,

00:12:56.439 --> 00:13:03.439
except maybe terrorism. And I had my own thoughts
on terrorism. I believed that the real threat

00:13:06.269 --> 00:13:13.269
was nationalist terrorism or communist terrorism,
whether it was PFLP, DFLP, PFLP-GC, some of

00:13:15.980 --> 00:13:20.250
the Greek groups, the Red Brigades, the Action
directe. You know, those were the groups that

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:26.329
were killing Americans overseas. Hezbollah--although
that was religious and not nationalist. That's

00:13:26.329 --> 00:13:30.470
where I thought the real threats were coming,
not the former Soviet Union. And that's what

00:13:30.470 --> 00:13:33.509
it was at that point: it was already the former
Soviet Union.

00:13:33.509 --> 00:13:39.100
JAY: Right. So you get in and very quickly
you're sent to Kuwait?

00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:46.100
KIRIAKOU: Well, I was given Kuwait as what
they called a training account. I was told,

00:13:47.779 --> 00:13:52.720
you know, Kuwait has this very interesting
democratic movement. People demonstrate every

00:13:52.720 --> 00:13:58.860
once in a while. They're experimenting with
a democratically elected parliament. So learn

00:13:58.860 --> 00:14:03.959
Kuwait, learn how to write in the CIA's analytic
style, and then you can move on to something

00:14:03.959 --> 00:14:09.759
really interesting, like Romania. That's what
the guy told me. So I thought, doggone it,

00:14:09.759 --> 00:14:14.089
I'm going to be stuck with this country where
nothing ever happens; I'm never going to get

00:14:14.089 --> 00:14:14.769
promoted.

00:14:14.769 --> 00:14:18.149
JAY: This is still based--you're still in
Arlington? Or you're actually in Kuwait?

00:14:18.149 --> 00:14:19.730
KIRIAKOU: No, I'm in Arlington, still.

00:14:19.730 --> 00:14:20.259
JAY: Yeah.

00:14:20.259 --> 00:14:25.769
KIRIAKOU: So I did Kuwait for four months.
And then, in April 1990, I also picked up

00:14:25.769 --> 00:14:32.769
Iraq. And at the time, in the CIA, you could
count on two hands the number of people that

00:14:33.699 --> 00:14:40.589
covered Iraq. And I mean in everything--analysis,
operations. Everything. Press. You name it.

00:14:40.589 --> 00:14:47.490
Again, I was told that Iraq is a good training
company. Nothing ever changes there. It's

00:14:47.490 --> 00:14:54.490
the same cabinet since 1968, right? No changes,
nothing happens, no trouble, no problems.

00:14:56.629 --> 00:14:57.660
I said, okay.

00:14:57.660 --> 00:15:03.540
JAY: Now, just to be clear for everybody,
you're in the analysis section, not the operations

00:15:03.540 --> 00:15:03.839
section.

00:15:03.839 --> 00:15:07.470
KIRIAKOU: No. We had very poor relations with
Iraq at the time, so our embassy was very,

00:15:07.470 --> 00:15:12.569
very small. It was something like four or
five people. There wasn't really much reporting

00:15:12.569 --> 00:15:17.639
coming out of it. I'd read the Iraqi papers
every day or translations of the Iraqi papers

00:15:17.639 --> 00:15:22.759
that weren't in English. And I was really
more focused on [Kuwait].

00:15:22.759 --> 00:15:28.990
And then, on August 2, 1990, Iraq invaded
Kuwait--I should say I was more focused on

00:15:28.990 --> 00:15:35.660
Kuwait. So Iraq invaded Kuwait. The night
before the invasion, we predicted that the

00:15:35.660 --> 00:15:38.999
Iraqis would of course cross the border.

00:15:38.999 --> 00:15:45.999
And I had a softball game that night. The
CIA has its own softball league. That way,

00:15:46.040 --> 00:15:51.209
if you say something classified, it's okay,
because everybody's a CIA employee. Right?

00:15:51.209 --> 00:15:58.209
So I had a softball game that night. And one
of the older guys came up to me and said,

00:15:59.709 --> 00:16:05.449
I don't think you understand how important
this Iraq-Kuwait thing is. He said, it's one

00:16:05.449 --> 00:16:06.269
thing for our country to go to war with another
country. Let me rephrase that. It's one thing

00:16:06.269 --> 00:16:10.149
for our countries to go to war with each other,
the countries that we cover. It's an entirely

00:16:10.149 --> 00:16:16.290
different thing for the country we cover to
go to war with us. He said, you have a chance

00:16:16.290 --> 00:16:23.290
here to really become somebody. So pay close
attention and do your analysis.

00:16:23.790 --> 00:16:30.790
JAY: And how much pressure was there on you,
next-Iraq-war style, to shape and modify your

00:16:31.269 --> 00:16:37.529
analysis in favor of Saddam's totally the
bad guy, our hands are clean?

00:16:37.529 --> 00:16:39.389
KIRIAKOU: Yeah, you mean in 2003?

00:16:39.389 --> 00:16:44.430
JAY: No, no, no. I'm talking back this. When
you're analyzing--like, for example--.

00:16:44.430 --> 00:16:44.889
KIRIAKOU: In 1990?

00:16:44.889 --> 00:16:50.319
JAY: Yeah, prior to the first Gulf War. It
later came out how much bullshit there was,

00:16:50.319 --> 00:16:54.899
like this infant that was supposedly--infants
being tortured story was apparently a fraud.

00:16:54.899 --> 00:17:00.839
There's pretty good documentation the American
ambassador to Iraq lets Saddam know--.

00:17:00.839 --> 00:17:05.329
KIRIAKOU: No, that's not true. I was there
that night. She got a bum rap on that. That

00:17:05.329 --> 00:17:06.670
was Ambassador April Glaspie.

00:17:06.670 --> 00:17:07.450
JAY: Right.

00:17:07.450 --> 00:17:12.750
KIRIAKOU: April Glaspie was instructed by
the secretary of state, who was James Baker

00:17:12.750 --> 00:17:19.050
at the time, to go to see Saddam and to tell
Saddam that the United States will not take

00:17:19.050 --> 00:17:23.510
a position on Arab-Arab border disputes. That's
all she said.

00:17:23.510 --> 00:17:29.170
JAY: No, don't think it's ever--any more than
that's ever been said that she said. The interpretation's

00:17:29.170 --> 00:17:31.490
been that we will not intervene.

00:17:31.490 --> 00:17:35.300
KIRIAKOU: Well, after the fact, the press
said that Saddam's interpretation was that

00:17:35.300 --> 00:17:39.710
that was a green light. But her instructions
were not to give him the green light.

00:17:39.710 --> 00:17:44.940
JAY: I understand. As I've always heard the
story, what you said is what she said.

00:17:44.940 --> 00:17:45.500
KIRIAKOU: Yeah.

00:17:45.500 --> 00:17:50.220
JAY: But there was never a suggestion that
we would intervene. Quite the opposite: this

00:17:50.220 --> 00:17:53.760
is your business between you and Kuwait, and
we're going to stay out of it. That was the--.

00:17:53.760 --> 00:17:59.090
KIRIAKOU: Nobody, nobody expected him to take
the entire country. Frankly, we expected that

00:17:59.090 --> 00:18:03.830
Iraqi troops would move across the border
and that they would take that narrow strip

00:18:03.830 --> 00:18:07.990
on the Kuwaiti side that included the southern
tip of the Rumaila oil field. That's what

00:18:07.990 --> 00:18:11.680
everybody expected to happen, 'cause, frankly,
the truth of the matter is that Kuwaitis were

00:18:11.680 --> 00:18:17.870
drilling horizontally. So they were drilling
in their little tip of the Rumaila oil field

00:18:17.870 --> 00:18:20.770
underneath the Iraqi border and they were
stealing the oil.

00:18:20.770 --> 00:18:22.030
JAY: Stealing Iraqi oil.

00:18:22.030 --> 00:18:23.460
KIRIAKOU: Stealing Iraqi oil.

00:18:23.460 --> 00:18:25.050
JAY: And you knew that at the time.

00:18:25.050 --> 00:18:30.400
KIRIAKOU: Everybody did, and it was in the
papers and everything. So everybody, including

00:18:30.400 --> 00:18:35.050
us, believed that the Iraqis would move across
the border, take the oilfield, and that would

00:18:35.050 --> 00:18:39.700
be the end of it. In fact, within 24 hours
they took the entire country and then just

00:18:39.700 --> 00:18:42.760
began murdering everybody.

00:18:42.760 --> 00:18:47.870
JAY: But some of the murder stories were exaggerated.
This murder of infants stuff didn't happen.

00:18:47.870 --> 00:18:51.440
KIRIAKOU: Yeah, I never understood why the
Kuwaitis felt compelled to exaggerate those

00:18:51.440 --> 00:18:56.820
stories, because there were so many despicable
crimes against humanity that Iraqi soldiers

00:18:56.820 --> 00:19:01.700
were committing in Kuwait. There was no reason
to lie about them. I remember when the girl

00:19:01.700 --> 00:19:07.550
that you're speaking about testified before
Congress. I remember watching that at the

00:19:07.550 --> 00:19:13.410
CIA and saying, I know that girl. Isn't she
the Kuwaiti ambassador's daughter? That's

00:19:13.410 --> 00:19:14.640
Saud Nasser's daughter.

00:19:14.640 --> 00:19:15.480
JAY: Right.

00:19:15.480 --> 00:19:20.210
KIRIAKOU: So, I mean, it only took about a
day or two to break the story that this whole

00:19:20.210 --> 00:19:21.520
thing was a setup.

00:19:21.520 --> 00:19:22.340
JAY: Right.

00:19:22.340 --> 00:19:29.340
KIRIAKOU: And, frankly, the Kuwaitis were
guilty of lying and trying to propagandize

00:19:30.970 --> 00:19:35.360
the situation. But so was their public relations
firm. I don't think the Kuwaitis came up with

00:19:35.360 --> 00:19:36.190
that on their own.

00:19:36.190 --> 00:19:40.090
JAY: And when you say everyone expected it
to come in and just take the oilfield, then

00:19:40.090 --> 00:19:43.890
it's not that much of a stretch for Saddam
to say, well, I have a green light to take

00:19:43.890 --> 00:19:45.280
the oilfield.

00:19:45.280 --> 00:19:52.110
KIRIAKOU: At the time, I would have said that
that was insane. I can see how he may have

00:19:52.110 --> 00:19:58.670
believed that he had a green light. But Ambassador
Glaspie certainly did not believe she was

00:19:58.670 --> 00:19:59.820
giving him a green light.

00:19:59.820 --> 00:20:06.150
JAY: 'Cause the overall suggestion is that
U.S. policymakers wanted Saddam to go--give

00:20:06.150 --> 00:20:10.570
him some rope to hang himself, because they
wanted an excuse to have the first war.

00:20:10.570 --> 00:20:15.870
KIRIAKOU: I never believed that, because Saddam
was the only thing that we had standing between

00:20:15.870 --> 00:20:21.840
Iran and the small Gulf countries. We needed
Saddam and the Iranians to just kill each

00:20:21.840 --> 00:20:23.020
other for decades.

00:20:23.020 --> 00:20:27.610
JAY: Well, that's the same argument made about
the next Saddam war, except that didn't stop

00:20:27.610 --> 00:20:28.290
the invasion.

00:20:28.290 --> 00:20:28.980
KIRIAKOU: Right.

00:20:28.980 --> 00:20:30.750
JAY: So one can wonder.

00:20:30.750 --> 00:20:36.710
KIRIAKOU: Yeah. Well, that just goes to show
you what a stupid policy we had in 2003, to

00:20:36.710 --> 00:20:43.710
provoke this war with the Iraqis, because
now we've created a regional hegemon in Iran.

00:20:44.370 --> 00:20:51.370
We took out an entire sect of Iraq, and we've
disenfranchised Sunnis, we've taken their

00:20:53.610 --> 00:20:59.620
positions, we've taken them out of government
and out of the military, and you have an entirely

00:20:59.620 --> 00:21:06.620
Shia military in Iraq now that is in some
ways supportive of Iran's theocracy and in

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:11.410
other ways accepting of it.

00:21:11.410 --> 00:21:16.950
JAY: We're going to carry on with your story
from Kuwait further in the next segment of

00:21:16.950 --> 00:21:17.320
our interview.

00:21:17.320 --> 00:21:22.250
Please join us for the continuation of Reality
Asserts Itself with John Kiriakou on The Real

00:21:22.250 --> 00:21:29.250
News Network.

